 ],
 "literal": "Those who say, 'Bear your love!' do they / not know that? Or do they possess such strength? / If we do not see our lover, / with a heart filled with dense grief, like the tiny foam / dashing against the rocks in the great ocean, / slowly, slowly, we will indeed cease to exist.",
 "translation": "Those who tell me to bear my love—do they not know what it is like, or are they themselves possessed of such immense strength? If I do not see my lover, then with a heart swollen with dense grief, like the tiny foam that dashes against the rocks in the great ocean, slowly, slowly, I will cease to exist.",
 "modern": "காதலைத் தாங்கிக் கொள் என்று சொல்பவர்கள், அதன் தன்மையை அறியாதவர்களோ? அல்லது அவ்வளவு வலிமை உடையவர்களோ? நம் காதலரைக் காணவில்லை என்றால், மிகுந்த துன்பம் பெருகிய நெஞ்சத்தோடு, பெரிய கடலின் பாறைகளில் மோதும் சிறு நுரையைப் போல, மெல்ல மெல்ல நாம் அழிந்து போவோம்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante in deep distress because her lover has been away for a long time. She questions the advice of onlookers who casually tell her to control her passion, wondering if they are completely ignorant of love's power or if they possess superhuman strength. She compares her fragile state to the tiny foam of the vast ocean that repeatedly dashes against hard rocks. This poignant image belongs to the Neytal (coastal) landscape, which represents the grief of waiting and separation. The key words are 'காமம்' (passion), 'சிறுநுரை' (tiny foam), and 'இல்லாகுதுமே' (we will cease to exist).",
